ility bond spreads widened from 130bp in Q1 to 135bp in Q2 and 165bp in Q3. That is a tightening trend, not a shut market.\n- Pellham probably can't cover its plan with debt alone, because its equity route has failed.\n\n**Equity**\n- Two Caldrian utility share offerings were cut back in Q3 2026.\n- Pellham, the largest utility, pulled its C$2.4B offering on 14 October.\n- Pellham says it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and resize its Q1 2027 tender, which was about C$900M. That suggests the equity shortfall is already forcing its capex down.\n- The 2027 capital plans were filed in October, before the pulled offering. The filed amounts probably overstate what Pellham will actually spend.\n\n**What this means**\n- 2027 spending plans are rising: Pellham plans an 18% increase and Norvane 12%. Those are the filed plans, though, not what will be spent.\n- For VLTX, Pellham's order cut is the main risk. Pellham was the largest single buyer, and VLTX won 60% of the last tender round.\n\n**Gaps and checks**\n- I did not do the research myself. This rests on two child nodes' summaries (n5 on debt, n6 on equity), which I read but did not check against the sources. I have not checked the bond-spread series myself.\n- Neither node found total capex dollar amounts, debt maturities, leverage or credit ratings. They also did not see Pellham's statement on how it will cover the shortfall, or Norvane's own funding position.\n- The evidence is thin: a few short documents.\n\nSources: n5.r1 (debt), n6.r1 (equity).","ttft_ms":1742,"type":"result","duration_ms":52734,"uuid":"0a7cac4f-c6a3-4dbc-bbb2-a499556391aa","ttft_stream_ms":1249,"time_to_request_ms":89,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":7,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":23,"input_hooks":4,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":12,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":7,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":0,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"auto